WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Let’s talk about closing the presentation

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.050 
I don’t want to end the presentation too abruptly 

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.300
just because I’m running out of time

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.100
So plan a “graceful exit” for your presentation

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.040 
Signal your audience that the presentation 

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.250
is coming to an end. You can say

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:24.100
“That brings me to the end of my presentation” 

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:30.000
or “If no one else has any questions, let’s stop here”

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.070
If they are interested in learning more about the subject

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.120 
you can provide additional information.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:41.000
Something like “You can read more about this

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.190 
via the links on the screen.” 

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.200
Or, “Here is a list of some readings on this topic.”

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.300
The audience will certainly appreciate the extra help

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.290
You might start feeling tired

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.250 
and exhausted toward the end

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.220
Remember to keep up your energy

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:04.100 
and enthusiasm until the last minute

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.300
Good reminder

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:09.300 
We want to start well and end well.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.140
You need to finish on a high note

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.040 
That means you should complete

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.210 
your presentation at a successful point

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.210 
Yes. A powerful ending should leave

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.100 
a lasting impression on your audience.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.200 
How about ending with a call to action?

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.000  
Sometimes we want the audience 

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:37.050 
to react and respond to our message

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.100  
and put words into action.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.210 
Right. Some of my favorite

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:46.050  
call-to-action lines are: “Join the fight,”

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.110  
“Take action” and “Make a difference.”

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:54.100 
Those are some strong and powerful action words

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.100  
That’s something an audience can take home with them.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.170 
Also, use positive action verbs

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.080  
such as “I urge you…”, “Get involved” 

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.100 
or “Partner with us.” 

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.100
You hope to receive feedback from your audience

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.230 
This kind of call to action will motivate them

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.200 
to respond to what they hear.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:17.200
That’s a good point

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:20.100
You should also thank your audience again

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.010
for their time and for paying attention 

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:24.200
to your presentation

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.200
Say something like

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.200 
I appreciate you giving me the time

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.080 
to share my ideas with you

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:34.100 
or Thank you for your kind attention

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:38.000 
or even a simple, Thank you for listening

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.100
That’s very good

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.100 
I am sure the audience would like to hear that

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:49.150 
It should make a perfect ending to your presentation

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.200
Thank you both for your suggestions and tips

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:55.200 
I am going back to work on my presentation now

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:58.100
I know it’ll be a good one

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.100
Yes. Now you are making a positive 

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:04.200
and encouraging ending to our conversation, too!